\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {2.3.1}Object-class-to-strategy and strategy-to-parameter mapping}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{eq:strategy-vector}{{9}{5}{}{equation.9}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsubsection}{\numberline {2.3.2}One-shot triggering, strategy locking, and parameter transition}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{eq:smoothstep-update}{{10}{5}{}{equation.10}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Strategy-level parameter settings and assigned objects. $K_t$ is reported in N/m, $K_r$ in N\,m/rad, $d$ in N, and $v_g$ in m/s. $F_g$ denotes the requested-force input to \texttt  {grasp()} in newtons. The damping-scaling parameter $\zeta $ and force-scaling factor $K_f$ are dimensionless.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:2}{{2}{6}{}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Object-conditioned strategy-level parameter-configuration architecture. D435i RGB frames were processed asynchronously by YOLO11n, and object-class labels and confidence scores were returned through bounded queues. The nominal 200-Hz supervisory loop polled the result queue without blocking. The first result with confidence $\geq 0.25$ triggered the object-class-to-strategy mapping and a one-shot update of the enabled slave-side impedance, master-side haptic, and gripper-execution parameter channels. The selected strategy remained locked for the trial. When no threshold-qualified result was available, the initialized parameter state was retained; a detected class without an explicit mapping invoked the balanced default strategy. The continuous operator--robot motion and force-feedback loop remained active throughout task execution.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:3}{{3}{6}{}{figure.3}{}}
